.TH PCB 5
.SH NAME
pcb \- board source and lock
.SH DESCRIPTION
The board source says in words where the parts go and how their copper
runs; it is the only file anyone edits.
Parts and pins have the names of the schematic (ch1.gain.rf,
ch1.gain.amp.out); there are no coordinates but those the mechanics
fix.
A statement a line, words separated by blanks; # starts a comment.
Lengths are mm, y up; angles degrees: the rot R of a fix or place
statement is 0, 90, 180 or 270.
.PP
.RS
.nf
netlist FILE                         the netlist(5) of schnet
board rect X0 Y0 X1 Y1               the outline
cutout rect X0 Y0 X1 Y1 [round R]  a hole through the board, edge
					   like the outline, its corners of
					   radius R (8 at most)
layer NAME signal | plane NET        the copper layers, top first
rule clearance D | track W | via DIA DRILL
rule viagap D | maskweb D
rule edge D [except PART...]
footprint MPN FP                     the footprint of a part number
                                     (or of a type, for a part without)
fix PART at X Y [rot R] [bottom]     where the mechanics put it
place PART WAY of PART [GAP] [rot R] next to the part, centered
place PART WAY from PIN [rot R]     its pad of the pin's net by the pin
place GROUP WAY from PIN             a block, whole, as one part of two sides
place PART|GROUP near THING [WAY] [GAP] [rot R]
                                     in the nearest room by THING
width NET W                          its tracks
width PIN PIN W                      the copper between two of its pins
path NET PIN PIN...                  its pins wired in this order
via PIN WAY                          the way its plane via goes
unplaced GROUP			   its parts not on the board yet
block GROUP                          the group laid out alone
ref PART SIDE [ALIGN] [rot 90] [size H]
                                   its designator by its courtyard
text TEXT at X Y [size H] [bottom]   text on the silkscreen
note TEXT                            for the readers
.fi
.RE
.PP
The width of the copper between two pins of a net is the width of a
statement naming them, else the net's, else the rule's.
The rules, in mm: clearance between copper of two nets (0.2); track,
the width of a track (0.2); via, a via's diameter and drill (0.6 0.3);
viagap, from the edge of a pad soldered (a pad with paste, or a plated
hole) to the ring of a via of its net, which a neck track joins to it
(0.3), so no via stands on or by its pad and the solder does not wick
down it; a contact pad, a footprint's pad without paste, is never
soldered, and a via may stand in it; maskweb, the
least web of solder mask between two openings, and between the opening
of a pad soldered and a via, which the mask covers (0.1); edge, from copper to the
outline and the cutouts (0.5), but for the parts it excepts.
.PP
A ref statement puts a part's designator on a SIDE of its courtyard
(above, below, left or right, at the gap the placer leaves), at the
start (the left or top end), middle (the default) or end of that side,
turned to read upward with rot 90, and H mm tall (0.5 to 0.8, 0.8 by
default), in the stroke font of
.IR vec (5),
its pen 0.15 of H wide (0.12 mm at 0.8), its box grown by the
descender for a text that has one.
A designator with no ref statement reads along its courtyard's long
side (turned to read upward for a part taller than wide), on whichever
long side has room, as large as fits, from 0.8 mm down to 0.5 by 0.1,
and at that size as near the courtyard as fits, from the gap the placer
leaves out by 0.1 mm to 0.95, centered on the side; failing both long
sides, a short side.
Room is clear of the mask openings and of the courtyards of the other
parts of its side, of the designators of its side placed before it,
and inside the board by the edge rule; the designators with the fewest
places clear (designators aside) choose first, each taking its room,
and one with no room takes its first place, which
.IR pcbcheck (1)
tells.
Every program computes them alike, and no part moves for them.
.PP
A near statement puts a part, or a block whole (its one part that no
statement places is its root), at the nearest spot where its courtyard
(a block's, the box of its parts' courtyards) is clear, searching out
from THING by 0.1 mm: THING is a part, a group (the box of its parts'
courtyards), a pin (its pad), a cutout (cutout N, the Nth of the
source, the first if N is not said) or the edge (the outline, the room
inside it); WAY keeps to that side of it, and GAP (in mm, 0 by default)
is the least distance from it; along a side, the spot nearest its
middle comes first.
Without rot a part stands unturned.
.PP
A text statement writes TEXT (its words, to the word at) on the top
silkscreen, or the bottom's (reading from below), in the stroke font of
.IR vec (5),
the left end of its baseline at X Y and its capitals H mm high (0.8 by
default).
.PP
An unplaced statement leaves a group's parts (every part whose name
begins with the group and a dot, a page's or any other's) off the board
until it is laid out: they take no footprint, so no pads, and the
programs place, route, plot and check the board without them; a net
with pins on them is whole among its placed pins alone, and
.IR pcbcheck (1)
notes the group and each such net.
.SS Blocks
A board is laid out by groups, the groups of the schematic (ch1,
ch1.gain): a block statement says a group is laid out alone, and
.I pcbplace
and
.I pcbroute
lay out each such group in one run, the deepest first, then the board
(or one group alone, with
.BR \-g ).
A group's lock is its section of the board's lock (group GROUP),
relative to the group's origin,
where its root stands: its part placed by no part of the group (fixed,
or placed against a part or pin outside it; placed from a pin, it lies
with its pad of the pin's net toward the pin, so the port faces its
neighbor).
Its nets whose pads are all its own are routed with it, and the vias
of its pads; the nets to its ports wait for the layout round it.
In any layout round it the block is one rigid body: its parts and its
copper as its section has them, moved, unturned, to where its root's statement
puts the root, and pushed out the root's way until clear.
A block placed by
.B place
.I GROUP WAY
.B from
.I PIN
stands as one part of two sides: its pads of the pin's net face the
pin, centered on it across the way, and its other side faces away; its
root is its one part that no statement places, its other parts placed
against each other only.
In a layout round it, a block's pads keep the clearance from the vias of
the other blocks.
So a board is made inside out: the leaf blocks, then the groups of
blocks, each its own section, then the board; and a block of the same
statements, renamed, has the same layout, copper for copper.
.SS Lock
The lock is what the programs resolved, beside the source: generated,
never edited, but kept, so its diff is the review of an edit.
It is one file: the board's section first, then a section for each
group laid out alone, each from its line
.B group
.I GROUP
to the next, in the order of their names, all in the same words; a
program reads its own section and writes it anew, the others as they
were.
.PP
.RS
.nf
part NAME REF X Y ROT top|bottom nets PAD NET ... by STATEMENT
net NAME by STATEMENTS
pad NET PART.PAD REF X Y W H top|bottom|all
link NET PART.PAD PART.PAD|via|plane W
track NET LAYER W X Y X Y...
via NET X Y DIA DRILL
.fi
.RE
.PP
All a program needs to tell what is stale is there in plain words.
A part is listed with its designator, where it stands, the net of each
of its pads (its turn depends on them) and the statement it was placed
by; a net with the statements its copper was made from (path, via,
joined by ;) and, a pad a line, each of its pads as it stood then:
its part and name, the part's designator, its center, its size along x
and y, and its side.
The copper is kept by connection: a link names the two pads it joins
(or a pad and its via, or a pad and its plane, by a neck) and its
width, and the tracks and vias after it, up to the next link or net, are
its copper; so an edit makes anew only the connections it touches: a
pad moved, a width changed, copper now in a moved part's way.
A pin of several pads names each by its place among them (j.2/1).
The programs keep what still matches and resolve the rest, and
.IR pcbcheck (1)
tells what is stale, and why: the statement changed, a pad's net
changed, or a pad moved.
The designators are for the reader: the programs read a part by its
name, so a new annotation changes no place and no copper.
The parts and the nets are in the order of their names, so the same
source and lock give the same bytes.
Deleting a line, or the lock, resolves that anew.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
fix ch1.jack.j at 93.5736 -141.224
place ch1.gain.amp up of ch1.jack.j 9 rot 270
place ch1.vpos.c down from ch1.gain.amp.v+
width ch1.term.out 0.635
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
pcbplace(1), pcbroute(1), pcbcheck(1), pcbgerber(1), pcbfp(5)
